\details{
Interception is on by default after \code{library(maidr)}: printing a ggplot2
or lattice object opens it in the MAIDR viewer, and Base R plotting calls
are recorded until \code{\link[=show]{show()}} is called. lattice is reached through its own
hook, \code{lattice.options(print.function = )}, which maidr sets once lattice's
namespace is loaded; a print function set before is kept, and draws the
prints maidr leaves to lattice.

In an R Markdown or Quarto document, \code{library(maidr)} is enough: loading
maidr during the knit, or else the first chart the document draws,
installs the knitr hooks that make every plot of it an accessible chart,
in each render of a session. So any document rendered in a session where
maidr is loaded, even only its namespace (as a package that imports maidr
loads it), gets charts, whether or not it loads maidr itself;
\code{options(maidr.auto_show = FALSE)} before the render prevents that. In a
document, \code{maidr_on()} installs the hooks at once.

In HTML output the charts are part of the page, which loads maidr.js once
for all of them, and the static figures of a chunk are recorded with
svglite rather than knitr's default png (\code{maidr.knitr_dev} in
\link{maidr-options} turns that off). A chunk can draw several charts --
\code{print(p)} in a loop, several Base R charts -- and each takes the place of
its own figure; a figure maidr cannot read as one chart stays knitr's
image. HTML that cannot hold a chart in the page, such as an HTML
fragment or pagedown, shows each chart in a frame of its own. In PDF,
Word, Markdown, EPUB or xaringan output the plots are knitr's figures, as
without maidr. A chart a document returns or prints never opens the
viewer; an explicit \code{\link[=show]{show()}} still does, during the render, and puts
nothing in the page.

A chart is drawn at its chunk's \code{fig.width} and \code{fig.height}, in inches,
as knitr draws the chunk's figures: \code{fig.asp} and \code{fig.dim} set them as
they do for a figure, and so do R Markdown's \code{fig_width} and \code{fig_height}
and Quarto's \code{fig-width} and \code{fig-height}. \code{html_document} and Quarto's
HTML formats draw figures at 7 x 5 in, maidr's own size outside a
document. A format with a figure size of its own draws its charts at
that size: \code{rmarkdown::html_vignette} at 3 x 3 in, small enough that a
Base R chart shows fewer tick labels, ioslides at 7.5 x 4.5 in, slidy at
8 x 6 in and \code{knitr::knit()} on its own at 7 x 7 in. Set \code{fig.width} and
\code{fig.height} to draw a chart at another size.

A chart is shown at the size knitr shows the chunk's figure: \code{fig.width}
times \code{dpi} over \code{fig.retina} pixels wide for a retina figure, 96 pixels
to the inch in R Markdown's and Quarto's HTML, where knitr's figures are
\code{png} at \code{dpi = 96} and \code{fig.retina = 2}; a pixel to a dot for any other
bitmap; and at the size its file gives an svg figure, 96 pixels to the
inch for svglite's and 72 for R's \code{svg()} with Cairo 1.18 (Quarto's
\code{fig-format: svg}). So \code{dpi}, Quarto's \code{fig-dpi}, changes it as it
changes a figure. Its SVG's \code{viewBox} is still 72 to the inch, the
canvas it was drawn on, and what maidr reads out is the same at any
size. \code{show()} and \code{save_html()} show a chart at 72 pixels to the inch.

A candlestick chart is drawn at least 12 x 6 in. A chunk that sets a
smaller size of its own is told so in a message (see \code{\link[=show]{show()}}): among the
chunk's messages for a ggplot2 chart, and on the console for a Base R
\code{chartSeries()} chart, which maidr reads once the chunk has run. The
document's figure size, which the format, its YAML or
\code{knitr::opts_chunk$set()} sets for every chunk, was not asked of a
candlestick chart, which is drawn larger than it without a message.

maidr draws a Base R chart again from its recorded calls, with the
margins and text size its chunk's \code{par()} calls set (\code{mar}, \code{mai},
\code{oma}, \code{omi}, \code{mex} and \code{cex}), so it fits where R drew it. A chart R
drew in its chunk with settings maidr does not record -- a \code{par()} called
by name, as \code{graphics::par()}, or another of its settings -- can still be
too small for its size in maidr. Too small for the document's figure
size, it is drawn larger, as one too small for 7 x 5 in is outside a
document (see \code{\link[=show]{show()}}), and a message on the console names the size.
Too small for a size its chunk sets of its own, it stays knitr's picture,
with a warning naming the size.

Whether a chunk set its size is read from the size, not from where it
was set: a chunk that sets the document's own, \code{fig.width = 7} and
\code{fig.height = 5} in \code{html_document}, is taken to set none, for a
candlestick chart and a Base R chart alike.

The size is the room the chart is laid out in, not what a reader hears:
its data, titles and axes are the same at every size, though a lattice
chart conditioned on one variable with no \verb{layout =} arranges its panels,
and the grid a reader moves through, for it (see \code{\link[=show]{show()}}).

\code{out.width} sets the width a chart is shown at, wider or narrower than
its own size, as it does a figure's, and \code{out.height} its height, though
a chart keeps its shape where an image is stretched; \code{fig.align} aligns
it. In Quarto a captioned chart is centred unless the chunk aligns it, a
percentage \code{out-width} in a \code{layout} panel is its cell's, and \code{column: screen} and its kin stretch a chart across the screen. A chart always
shrinks to fit a page narrower than itself, keeping its shape: Quarto's
\code{fig-responsive: false}, which keeps an image from shrinking, is not
read, so that a reader on a phone or with the page magnified need not
scroll sideways through a chart.

In a Quarto reveal.js deck a chart alone on a slide is stretched to the
room left on the slide, as Quarto stretches a lone figure, less the room
maidr's instructions, or a line of its text and its braille field, take
below it, so that they stay on the slide; it is a little smaller than the
figure for that. \code{auto-stretch} is read from the document's own front
matter, not from a project's \verb{_quarto.yml}; a slide marked \code{.nostretch},
a chart given an \code{out-height}, one in columns, a panel or a fragment, and
a phone's scroll view are left alone, as Quarto leaves an image. An
ioslides chart too large to leave that room on its slide is made smaller.
In a Quarto dashboard a chart fills its card as an image does, less a
line for maidr's text below it; the card scrolls to maidr's braille and
review fields. The size a chart is given on a slide or in a card does not
change while a reader uses it.

A chart's caption, in R Markdown and bookdown, is plain text: Markdown,
maths and \verb{\\@ref()} in \code{fig.cap} are shown as written, and a bookdown text
reference (\code{fig.cap = "(ref:label)"}) brings them in. \code{fig.show = "animate"} stays knitr's animation.

A chunk cached with \code{cache = TRUE} brings its charts back from knitr's
cache. One cached with \code{cache = 1} or \code{cache = 2} shows them only when its
code runs: rendered again from the cache, its figures are knitr's static
images, since the charts they were drawn from are not cached with them.
}
